#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115153 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115321 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdaed02f-09f2-5572-a70c-0e6979ef2167" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For the Union of Two Opposite-Sided Linear Inequalities that Overlap"}] :question/id #uuid "abae2e99-53b7-5d81-860c-9555ce50ec3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-965" :question/problem "Find all the values of $x$ that satisfy $12x+8 > 44$ or $5x+3 \\leq 28$." :question/worked-solution "We start by solving $12x+8 > 44$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}12x + 8 & > 44 \\\\ 12x & > 36 \\\\ x & > 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n![](images/e9/e9eec85ca2dd2a9fd01db97a5f281d16fd958e143e048f7ff3c5e7a87dd33f0a.png)\n\nThen, we solve\n\n$$\n5x+3 \\leq 28\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5x + 3 & \\le 28 \\\\ 5x & \\le 25 \\\\ x & \\le 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n![](images/c9/c9cec5cfc0e476fffe4f89f89ff0585992855e8e36fe09778029d5accd2bdc6b.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/dd/dd1330c9f2d9dc2140a3e3a4dc37ac269d134c5e367f59c9985ce04d4acd6304.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $-\\infty < x < \\infty$. We can write this using interval notation as $(-\\infty,\\infty)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115154 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91a5169c-6a92-535d-85e1-8b9363b49274" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Union of Two Linear Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "81405a9f-882c-50fb-8057-41bb54515d14" :question/math-academy-id "e-966" :question/problem "Find the set of values of $x$ that satisfy $6x-1 \\leq 2$ or $5x-1 > 9$. Express your answer in interval notation." :question/worked-solution "We start by solving\n\n$$\n6x-1 \\leq 2\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6x - 1 & \\le 2 \\\\ 6x & \\le 3 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{3}{6} \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n![](images/dd/dd2c101e02e14a147371bc1b822f3bc86081dd0059ac50ae1f9131909e73e3ad.png)\n\nThen, we solve $5x -1 > 9$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5x - 1 & > 9 \\\\ 5x & > 10 \\\\ x & > 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n![](images/8b/8b252e1c42dcac87072a33b33460a887b6ec3bfa0d1511f05a004935df1911ca.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/ac/ac44f335b135463820ec0db9bc04470769fee7aabc8717edcc351893b6d8de47.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\nx \\leq \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nor $x >2$. We can write this using interval notation as\n\n$$\n(- ∞, \\frac{1}{2}]∪(2, ∞)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115155 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e3a4ead-02d0-5023-a7a7-3aaaca19b563" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Union of Two Linear Inequalities When One Inequality is Compound"}] :question/id #uuid "dced871e-e24d-5d06-a4bb-9faa933ecac7" :question/math-academy-id "e-967" :question/problem "Find the set of values of $x$ that satisfy $1 < 5x+6 \\leq 11$ or $5x-7 > 8$." :question/worked-solution "We start by solving\n\n$$\n1 < 5x+6 \\leq 11\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & < 5x + 6 \\le 11 \\\\ -5 & < 5x \\le 5 \\\\ -1 & < x \\le 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n![](images/69/69948e87bc893a2461ec6e8bb77dd72e77e6d496970e31df899400c42238cd01.png)\n\nThen, we solve $5x-7 > 8$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5x - 7 & > 8 \\\\ 5x & > 15 \\\\ x & > 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n![](images/10/10bb9a3bcc0b13b015649e2dae70a3ff3dcd361f46e6c545182031d6e095ad16.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/84/84a05cd47818c9609609c2f02bd026ccadfa77c6641dff82936630bf04fbe77e.png)\n\nThus, the final solution is\n\n$$\n-1 < x \\leq 1\n$$\n\nor $x > 3$. We can write this using interval notation as\n\n$$\n(-1,1] \\cup (3,\\infty)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e3a4ead-02d0-5023-a7a7-3aaaca19b563" :knowledge-poin